This study examines a parameter-dependent elliptic system defined on $\R^n$ ($n \geq 3$):
\begin{align*}
\begin{cases}
-\Delta u_i = f_{i}(u_{i}) & \mbox{in } \R^n \\
(-\Delta u_{i},u_{i})=\lambda_{i}\|u_{i}\|^{2} & \mbox{in }\R^n
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
Our focus lies on scenarios where $f_{i}$ lacks classical coercivity conditions. Leveraging concentration compactness principles, we demonstrate that for sufficiently large parameter values, the system admits a ground state solution, with the energy of this solution bounded above by that of the associated decoupled system.

This work examines a specific class of bounded stop problem associated with the following equation:

$$
\begin{aligned}
dy_t &= -\left(g(y_t, z_t, u_t) + \lambda_t\right)dt - \sigma\lambda_t dw_t - \sigma z_t dW_t,
\end{aligned}
$$

where the term $g(y_t,z_t,u_t)$ is monotonic in $y_t$ and convex and concave in $u_t$. The study investigates how this problem behaves under an underlying diffusion process. It establishes the representation of the optimal value function and describes a variational inequality system that any optimal control must satisfy. Notably, the formulation of the problem incorporates both non-zero initial conditions for terminal data and constraints imposed by the underlying diffusion. Furthermore, the work addresses optimization scenarios where $g$ exhibits the triple obstacle structure, being the difference of two convex functions with respect to $u_t$. The authors represent the optimal control using a variational inequality system involving four obstacles defined by two continuous semimartingales, and the case of a triple obstacle structure involves a similar system with obstacles derived from $g$.

